<!-- Introductory Lead -->
<div class="space-y-4 mb-6 lg:mb-10 text-lg md:text-xl leading-relaxed">
  <p>
    The ongoing digitalisation of sport is giving rise to <strong>new sporting formats and disciplines</strong> (for example, the <a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" href="https://www.uci.org/discipline/cycling-esports/5awP766tEGOobRlwSdHBDs?discipline=CES">UCI's cycling esports</a>) that blur the boundaries between traditional sports and video gaming. 
    Rather than treating these formats as mere games or temporary trends, this page outlines why it makes sense to <strong>recognise and understand them as new distinct sporting disciplines</strong> that are here to stay.
    In what follows, we discuss <strong>what virtual sports are, why they matter, whom they impact, and how their future is taking shape</strong>.
  </p>
  <p>
    To make sense of virtual sports as emerging digitalised sporting disciplines, we link back to foundational concepts developed in social sciences and sport management.
    Most of the items explained on this page have been <strong>developed as part of <a href="/members/thibault-fouquaert">Thibault Fouquaert's</a> doctoral dissertation</strong> (Ghent University, Belgium) on the topic of virtual sports.
    Those who want to read or explore a more comprehensive and scientific analysis of virtual sports and some of the concepts discussed below can consult the <a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" href="https://biblio.ugent.be/publication/01KWESKD8T405VTFE3PADRE2JB">full open access text (UGent Biblio)</a> of Thibault's dissertation online.
  </p>
</div>

<!-- Section 1: Concept -->
<section class="mb-16 lg:mb-24 text-lg md:text-xl leading-relaxed">
  
  <div class="mb-8 lg:mb-10">
    <h2 class="text-3xl md:text-4xl font-bold {{ colors.headingsCustom or colors.headingsDefault }} tracking-tight">
      Concept
    </h2>
  </div>

  <div class="grid grid-cols-1 lg:grid-cols-12 gap-4 lg:gap-8 items-start">
    
    <!-- Left Column: Narrative Content & Dimensions -->
    <div class="lg:col-span-7 space-y-8 lg:text-pretty">
      
      <!-- Subsection 1 Header -->
      <div class="space-y-4">
        <h3 class="text-2xl md:text-3xl font-bold {{ colors.headingsCustom or colors.headingsDefault }} tracking-tight leading-tight">
          Using the Dimensions of Sport to Define Virtual Sports
        </h3>
        <p class="mt-4">
          To identify what sets virtual sports apart, we can analyse them across nine dimensions that characterise traditional sports.
          These dimensions were developed in sport sociology literature (e.g. Guttmann, 2004; Loy, 1968) and later extended by Fouquaert (2026) to analyse sport digitalisation. 
          They provide a structure to compare the activities of virtual sports, esports and exergames, relative to each other and to traditional sports. 
        </p>
      </div>

      <!-- The Nine Dimensions List -->
      <ol class="space-y-4 lg:space-y-6 leading-relaxed" role="list">
        
        <!-- Dimension 1 -->
        <li class="flex items-start gap-4">
          <span class="flex-none flex items-center justify-center w-8 h-8 rounded-full bg-vs-blue-900 text-white font-bold text-sm shadow-xs">
            1
          </span>
          <div class="text-base md:text-lg">
            <strong class="font-bold text-vs-blue-900 block sm:inline sm:mr-1">Human Agency:</strong>
            The outcome is determined by human participation and decision-making and not, for instance, by autonomous or agentic technology.
          </div>
        </li>

        <!-- Dimension 2 -->
        <li class="flex items-start gap-4 ">
          <span class="flex-none flex items-center justify-center w-8 h-8 rounded-full bg-vs-blue-900 text-white font-bold text-sm shadow-xs">
            2
          </span>
          <div class="text-base md:text-lg">
            <strong class="font-bold text-vs-blue-900 block sm:inline sm:mr-1">Voluntary Participation:</strong>
            Engagement is non-coercive and motivated by intrinsic or extrinsic reasons, such as enjoyment, health, community belonging, or competitive ambition.
          </div>
        </li>

        <!-- Dimension 3 -->
        <li class="flex items-start gap-4 ">
          <span class="flex-none flex items-center justify-center w-8 h-8 rounded-full bg-vs-blue-900 text-white font-bold text-sm shadow-xs">
            3
          </span>
          <div class="text-base md:text-lg">
            <strong class="font-bold text-vs-blue-900 block sm:inline sm:mr-1">Competition:</strong>
            Contests that test participants' capabilities against each other.
          </div>
        </li>

        <!-- Dimension 4 -->
        <li class="flex items-start gap-4 ">
          <span class="flex-none flex items-center justify-center w-8 h-8 rounded-full bg-vs-blue-900 text-white font-bold text-sm shadow-xs">
            4
          </span>
          <div class="text-base md:text-lg">
            <strong class="font-bold text-vs-blue-900 block sm:inline sm:mr-1">Physicality:</strong>
            Meaning that the physical engagement, in any form or way, of human beings is crucial to the outcomes of the activity.
          </div>
        </li>

        <!-- Dimension 5 -->
        <li class="flex items-start gap-4 ">
          <span class="flex-none flex items-center justify-center w-8 h-8 rounded-full bg-vs-blue-900 text-white font-bold text-sm shadow-xs">
            5
          </span>
          <div class="text-base md:text-lg">
            <strong class="font-bold text-vs-blue-900 block sm:inline sm:mr-1">Cognitivity:</strong>
            Skills involving real-time strategic thinking, tactical pacing, and situational awareness that complement participants’ physical prowess during the activity.
          </div>
        </li>

        <!-- Dimension 6 -->
        <li class="flex items-start gap-4">
          <span class="flex-none flex items-center justify-center w-8 h-8 rounded-full bg-vs-blue-900 text-white font-bold text-sm shadow-xs">
            6
          </span>
          <div class="text-base md:text-lg">
            <strong class="font-bold text-vs-blue-900 block sm:inline sm:mr-1">Following:</strong>
           A widespread activity that attracts audiences that extend beyond local interests or communities. For instance, a game played and known by only a handful of people can therefore not be considered sport.
          </div>
        </li>

        <!-- Dimension 7 -->
        <li class="flex items-start gap-4">
          <span class="flex-none flex items-center justify-center w-8 h-8 rounded-full bg-vs-blue-900 text-white font-bold text-sm shadow-xs">
            7
          </span>
          <div class="text-base md:text-lg">
            <strong class="font-bold text-vs-blue-900 block sm:inline sm:mr-1">Governance:</strong>
            A structure surrounding the activity that relies on formal established rules enforced by legitimate governing bodies such as international and national federations.
          </div>
        </li>

        <!-- Dimension 8 -->
        <li class="flex items-start gap-4">
          <span class="flex-none flex items-center justify-center w-8 h-8 rounded-full bg-vs-blue-900 text-white font-bold text-sm shadow-xs">
            8
          </span>
          <div class="text-base md:text-lg">
            <strong class="font-bold text-vs-blue-900 block sm:inline sm:mr-1">Institutionalisation:</strong>
            A status that is achieved through legitimacy, rules and norms, securing the role of sport as a significant social practice.
          </div>
        </li>

        <!-- Dimension 9 -->
        <li class="flex items-start gap-4">
          <span class="flex-none flex items-center justify-center w-8 h-8 rounded-full bg-vs-blue-900 text-white font-bold text-sm shadow-xs">
            9
          </span>
          <div class="text-base md:text-lg">
            <strong class="font-bold text-vs-blue-900 block sm:inline sm:mr-1">Reality:</strong>
            The specific environment and laws (i.e. nature, governing...) under which an activity takes place. For activities without any form of digitalisation (e.g. traditional sports), this is the actual, real-world reality. 
          </div>
        </li>

      </ol>

      <!-- Synthesis / Key Takeaways -->
      <div class="space-y-4">
        <p>
          While each dimension is more thoroughly discussed in the work of Fouquaert (<a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class="hover:bg-vs-blue-100/30" href="https://biblio.ugent.be/publication/01KWESKD8T405VTFE3PADRE2JB">2026</a>, p. 74), applying these dimensions of traditional sports to other digitalised sports activities leads to the following conclusions:
        </p>
        <ol class="space-y-3 pl-1 leading-relaxed" role="list">
          <li class="flex items-start gap-3">
            <span class="font-bold text-vs-blue-900 shrink-0">1)</span>
            <span>Virtual sports <strong>can be considered sports</strong> as they can fulfill each dimension of sport.</span>
          </li>
          <li class="flex items-start gap-3">
            <span class="font-bold text-vs-blue-900 shrink-0">2)</span>
            <span>Because not all dimensions are fulfilled in the exact same manner, it is necessary to clearly <strong>distinguish virtual sports</strong> from sports, esports and exergames.</span>
          </li>
          <li class="flex items-start gap-3">
            <span class="font-bold text-vs-blue-900 shrink-0">3)</span>
            <span>Virtual sports differentiate themselves from sports and esports in their <strong>physicality and reality</strong>, fulfilling these dimensions differently. More on that below 👇</span>
          </li>
        </ol>
      </div>
    </div>

