  <!-- Subsection 2: Core Distinctions (Movement Decompression & Interreality) -->
  <div class="mt-10 lg:mt-14 space-y-6">
    <div class="space-y-4">
      <h3 class="text-2xl md:text-3xl font-bold {{ colors.headingsCustom or colors.headingsDefault }} tracking-tight leading-tight">
        Where Virtual Sports Differ
      </h3>
      <p>
        Based on the dimensions above, Virtual Sports fulfill two dimensions so distinctly that it clearly distinguishes them from traditional sports and esports. To understand the unique physicality of virtual sports, we use the concept of <strong>Movement (de)compression</strong>. To understand the unique reality of virtual sports, we use the concept of <strong>Physical-Digital Interreality</strong>. Combined, they make up an activity that is not exactly sports, nor esports.
      </p>
    </div>

    <!-- Core Dimensions List with SVGs -->
    <ul class="space-y-6 lg:space-y-8" role="list">
      
      <!-- Distinction 1: Movement Decompression -->
      <li class="p-6 sm:p-8 rounded-2xl border border-slate-200/80 bg-white shadow-xs flow-root lg:text-justify">
        <!-- Header: Title & Subtitle -->
        <div class="mb-4">
          <h4 class="text-xl sm:text-2xl font-bold {{ colors.headingsCustom or colors.headingsDefault }} tracking-tight">
            Movement Decompression
          </h4>
          <div class="mt-1 flex items-center flex-wrap gap-1.5 text-base sm:text-lg font-normal text-slate-500">
            <span>Based on dimension</span>
            <span class="inline-flex items-center justify-center w-5 h-5 rounded-full bg-vs-blue-900 text-white font-bold text-xs shadow-sm">
              4
            </span>
            <span class="font-medium text-slate-700">Physicality</span>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- SVG Graphic (floated right on desktop, aligned with paragraph 1) -->
        <figure class="my-4 lg:my-0 lg:float-right lg:w-3/5 lg:pl-6 flex flex-col items-center justify-center lg:py-2 pt-0 bg-white">
          {% svg "movement-compression", 
                 "w-full h-auto max-h-[340px] select-none", 
                 "Diagram contrasting fine-motor compressed movement in esports with authentic gross-motor decompressed movement in virtual sports" %}
          <figcaption class="mt-2 text-sm text-slate-500 text-center">
            Source: Fouquaert (<a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class="hover:bg-vs-blue-100/30" href="https://biblio.ugent.be/publication/01KWESKD8T405VTFE3PADRE2JB">2026</a>, p. 78).
          </figcaption>
        </figure>

        <!-- Narrative Paragraphs (wrap around figure and expand full width below) -->
        <p class="text-base sm:text-lg leading-relaxed mb-4">
          Within esports, there is often a discussion and reluctance to consider it as sports because it is argued that esports "lack great physicality" (Jenny et al., <a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class="hover:bg-vs-blue-100/30" href="https://doi.org/10.1080/00336297.2016.1144517">2017</a>, p. 15) and are "non-physical" (Parry &amp; Giesbrecht, <a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class="hover:bg-vs-blue-100/30" href="https://doi.org/10.1080/00948705.2023.2216883">2023</a>, p. 210). However, others like Naraine (<a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class="hover:bg-vs-blue-100/30" href="https://doi.org/10.18060/24812">2022</a>, p. 40) argue that "esports <em>is</em> physical" given that other (traditional) sports like shooting require high-valued fine-motoric movements.
        </p>
        <p class="text-base sm:text-lg leading-relaxed mb-4">
          To better understand physicality in the context of (e)sports, it clearly becomes essential to look past non-physical versus physical, or fine-motoric versus gross-motoric.
          The concept of <strong>movement (de)compression</strong> by Hemmingsen (<a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class="hover:bg-vs-blue-100/30" href="https://doi.org/10.1080/16138171.2023.2259176">2024</a>) provides a solution to that. In essence, it considers <strong>physicality in relation to the movements required in the traditional sport and how well these are mimicked</strong>.
        </p>
        <p class="text-base sm:text-lg leading-relaxed mb-0">
          In esports, movements are highly <em>compressed</em>, meaning that the original movements from the parent sport are in a sense reduced to different (but not inferior) physical movements (e.g. accurately pressing buttons). 
          Applying movement (de)compression to virtual sports makes clear that <strong>esports and virtual sports are actually different in their physicality</strong>. Compared to esports, virtual sports clearly demonstrate highly <em>decompressed</em> movements (i.e. little <em>compressed</em>), mimicking the traditional "parent" sport with great accuracy and sensitivity. For example, the TGL SoFi simulator tracks the path of a golf club with millimeter precision.
        </p>
      </li>

      <!-- Distinction 2: Physical-Digital Interreality -->
      <li class="p-6 sm:p-8 rounded-2xl border border-slate-200/80 bg-white shadow-xs flow-root lg:text-justify">
        <!-- Header: Title & Subtitle -->
        <div class="mb-4">
          <h4 class="text-xl sm:text-2xl font-bold {{ colors.headingsCustom or colors.headingsDefault }} tracking-tight">
            Physical-Digital Interreality
          </h4>
          <div class="mt-1 flex items-center flex-wrap gap-1.5 text-base sm:text-lg font-normal text-slate-500">
            <span>Based on dimension</span>
            <span class="inline-flex items-center justify-center w-5 h-5 rounded-full bg-vs-blue-900 text-white font-bold text-xs shadow-sm">
              9
            </span>
            <span class="font-medium text-slate-700">Reality</span>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- SVG Graphic (floated right on desktop, aligned with paragraph 1) -->
        <figure class="my-4 lg:my-0 lg:float-right lg:w-1/2 lg:pl-6 flex flex-col items-center justify-center lg:py-2 pt-0 bg-white">
          {% svg "physical-digital-interreality", 
                 "w-full h-auto max-h-[340px] select-none", 
                 "Diagram illustrating physical-digital interreality and algorithmic mediation of physiological output in virtual sports" %}
          <figcaption class="mt-2 text-sm text-slate-500 text-center">
            Source: Fouquaert (<a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class="hover:bg-vs-blue-100/30" href="https://biblio.ugent.be/publication/01KWESKD8T405VTFE3PADRE2JB">2026</a>, p. 82).
          </figcaption>
        </figure>

        <!-- Narrative Paragraphs (wrap around figure and expand full width below) -->
        <p class="text-base sm:text-lg leading-relaxed mb-4">
          Activities like virtual sports or esports involve a digital translation of performances (e.g. human input mediated through a game engine).
          In such instances, it is important to <strong>recognise the potential impact of this digital translation</strong>. 
          If the outcome includes elements beyond solely human input (e.g. game engine algorithms), the sporting activity and its play becomes a hybrid outcome of what happens in the actual world and what happens in the digital world.  
        </p>
        <p class="text-base sm:text-lg leading-relaxed mb-4">
        This potential <strong>impact was previously not recognised</strong>, as virtual sports games were considered "simulations in sport" with "digital measurements <em>representing</em> the physical output of the athlete competing in the sport" (Parry &amp; Giesbrecht, <a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class="hover:bg-vs-blue-100/30" href="https://doi.org/10.1080/00948705.2023.2216883">2023</a>, p. 221). In fact, it was even argued that games do not "fundamentally change the nature of the sporting activity" (Parry &amp; Giesbrecht, <a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class="hover:bg-vs-blue-100/30" href="https://doi.org/10.1080/00948705.2023.2216883">2023</a>, p. 221).
        </p>
        <p class="text-base sm:text-lg leading-relaxed mb-0">
          The concept of <strong>physical-digital interreality</strong> by Fouquaert (<a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class="hover:bg-vs-blue-100/30" href="https://biblio.ugent.be/publication/01KWESKD8T405VTFE3PADRE2JB">2026</a>, p. 79) <strong>acknowledges this new hybrid reality</strong> based on research on esports and based on evidence on virtual cycling by Westmattelmann et al. (<a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class="hover:bg-vs-blue-100/30" href="https://doi.org/10.3389/fphys.2022.868902">2022</a>, <a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class="hover:bg-vs-blue-100/30" href="https://doi.org/10.1080/07421222.2025.2455774">2025</a>).
         These studies put forward empirical evidence of how measured physical performance (input) is not the only element that translates (100%) to the digital competition outcome (output).
         In virtual sports, the outcome is also determined by virtual, non-real, features and mechanisms such as virtual drafting dynamics, latency, gamification elements, avatars.
          As a result, the outcome of a virtual sport depends on both human input and digital alteration of such input. This makes clear that <strong>sports and virtual sports are actually different in their reality</strong>. Combined with the difference in Movement Decompression, virtual sports can simply not be considered as sports nor as esports, but present a new distinct category. 
        </p>
      </li>

    </ul>
  </div>

  <!-- Subsection 3: Towards a Universal Definition of Virtual Sports -->
  <div class="mt-10 lg:mt-14 space-y-6 text-lg md:text-xl leading-relaxed lg:text-pretty">
    <div class="space-y-4">
      <h3 class="text-2xl md:text-3xl font-bold {{ colors.headingsCustom or colors.headingsDefault }} tracking-tight leading-tight">
        Towards a Universal Definition of Virtual Sports
      </h3>
      <div class="flow-root">
        <!-- SVG Graphic (floated right on desktop, aligned with paragraph) -->
        <figure class="my-4 lg:my-0 lg:float-right lg:w-2/5 lg:pl-6 flex flex-col items-center justify-center lg:py-2 pt-0 bg-white">
          {% svg "virtuality-virtual-sports", 
                 "w-full h-auto max-h-[225px] select-none", 
                 "Diagram illustrating virtuality in sport and its operational dimensions including representational fidelity, remoteness, and synchrony" %}
          <figcaption class="mt-2 text-sm text-slate-500 text-center">
            Source: Adapted from Fouquaert (<a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class="hover:bg-vs-blue-100/30" href="https://biblio.ugent.be/publication/01KWESKD8T405VTFE3PADRE2JB">2026</a>, p. 11).
          </figcaption>
        </figure>

        <p>
          Within a definition of virtual sports, it is also essential to acknowledge that sport becomes <strong>digitally measured (i.e. virtuality)</strong>, and how this allows to organise sport in different ways. 
          Most importantly is to recognise that virtual sports are not bound to a single setup. 
          For instance, virtual sports can visually translate (i.e. representational fidelity) to a text-based online ranking, a 3D virtual world (e.g. Zwift), or to physical infrastructure using LED-integration (e.g. Mixed-Reality Table Tennis). 
          They can take place co-located on a physical event location (e.g. UCI Cycling Esports World Championships) or distributed (e.g. from-home online events like MyWhoosh Sunday Raceclub). 
          Lastly, virtual sports can be asynchronous or decoupled from time (e.g. Virtual Golf), or real-time synchronous events (e.g. competing simultaneously online against others). 
        </p>
      </div>
      <p>
        Based on the outline above, including the various dimensions and ways of organising virtual sports, an approach can be made to <strong>universally define virtual sports</strong>:
      </p>

      <!-- Scientific Definition Blockquote -->
      <blockquote class="relative my-8 sm:my-10 p-6 sm:p-8 md:p-10 max-w-4xl mx-auto rounded-2xl bg-slate-50 border border-slate-200/80 shadow-sm">
        <!-- Big Quotation Symbol overlapping top border -->
        <div class="absolute -top-6 left-6 sm:left-8 flex items-center justify-center w-12 h-12 rounded-full bg-vs-blue-900 text-white shadow-xs ring-4 ring-white" aria-hidden="true">
          {% svg "quote", "w-6 h-6", "Quotation mark" %}
        </div>

        <p class="text-lg md:text-xl leading-relaxed italic font-serif text-pretty">
          &ldquo;Institutionalised, rule-governed contests of human physical and cognitive skills that operate within a distinct physical-digital interreality that are characterized by voluntary human participation, a niche following, and high movement decompression mimicking a traditional parent sport, made possible through virtuality yet unbound to specific configurations of remoteness, (a)synchrony or representational fidelity.&rdquo;
        </p>
        <footer class="mt-4 not-italic text-sm sm:text-base font-semibold text-slate-600 flex items-center gap-2 font-sans">
          <span class="text-vs-blue-900 font-bold">Fouquaert (<a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class="hover:bg-vs-blue-100/30" href="https://biblio.ugent.be/publication/01KWESKD8T405VTFE3PADRE2JB">2026</a>, p. 84)</span>
        </footer>
      </blockquote>

      <p>
        This definition is academically developed and primarily aimed towards knowledge development. 
        It provides researchers, sport governing bodies, and policymakers with a robust outline of which activities could be considered virtual sports. 
        Having a solid understanding and frame of reference is essential to discuss the topics and make decisions about it.
        At the same time, such an academic definition is limited in practical terms. That is why the Virtual Sports Research Network, in the future, aims to develop a more practical definition through collaboration with platform developers, hardware manufacturers, and industry stakeholders, in order to support the further growth of virtual sports within the industry.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- Section 2: Relevance -->
<section id="relevance" class="mb-16 lg:mb-24 text-lg md:text-xl leading-relaxed">
  <div class="mb-8 lg:mb-10">
    <h2 class="text-3xl md:text-4xl font-bold {{ colors.headingsCustom or colors.headingsDefault }} tracking-tight">
      Relevance
    </h2>
  </div>

  <div class="space-y-6 lg:text-pretty">
    <p>
      Virtual sports represent a promising development within the sporting industry. 
      When approached strategically and well informed, they can create distinct strategic value for different stakeholders:
    </p>

    <!-- Stakeholder Relevance List -->
    <ul class="space-y-4 lg:space-y-6 leading-relaxed" role="list">
      
      <!-- Policymakers -->
      <li class="flex items-start gap-4">
        <span class="flex-none mt-2 flex items-center justify-center w-5 h-5 rounded-full bg-vs-blue-900 shadow-2xs" aria-hidden="true">
          <span class="w-2 h-2 rounded-full bg-vs-yellow-400"></span>
        </span>
        <div class="text-base md:text-lg">
          <strong class="font-bold text-vs-blue-900 block sm:inline sm:mr-1">Policymakers:</strong>
          Virtual sports serve as a strategic asset to advance both elite-oriented and Sport-for-All sport policies.
          They can take away barriers like weather constraints, safety issues, or lack of infrastructure. 
          Conversely, they can enable time-efficient, at-home, and accessible participation pathways for those who might otherwise remain physically inactive.
        </div>
      </li>

      <!-- National & International Sport Governing Bodies -->
      <li class="flex items-start gap-4">
        <span class="flex-none mt-2 flex items-center justify-center w-5 h-5 rounded-full bg-vs-blue-900 shadow-2xs" aria-hidden="true">
          <span class="w-2 h-2 rounded-full bg-vs-yellow-400"></span>
        </span>
        <div class="text-base md:text-lg">
          <strong class="font-bold text-vs-blue-900 block sm:inline sm:mr-1">National &amp; International Sport Governing Bodies:</strong>
          For policymakers and federations, virtual sports provide a platform to expand competitive sport offerings and formats.
          They offer new pathways toward internationally recognised competitions such as official World Championships or the (expected) Olympic Esports Games. New disciplines can be developed in which athletes and organisations can specialise and become professional.
        </div>
      </li>

      <!-- Voluntary Sports Clubs & Coaches -->
      <li class="flex items-start gap-4">
        <span class="flex-none mt-2 flex items-center justify-center w-5 h-5 rounded-full bg-vs-blue-900 shadow-2xs" aria-hidden="true">
          <span class="w-2 h-2 rounded-full bg-vs-yellow-400"></span>
        </span>
        <div class="text-base md:text-lg">
          <strong class="font-bold text-vs-blue-900 block sm:inline sm:mr-1">Voluntary Sports Clubs &amp; Coaches:</strong>
          At the grassroots level, clubs can innovate their services and attract new members by offering virtual rides and online structured workouts or club competitions.
          As explained in <a class="hover:bg-vs-blue-100/30" href="/2025/12/18/virtual-cycling-in-sports-clubs-how-does-that-fit-in-existing-sport-policy/">our blogpost</a>, virtual sports can be leveraged for performance-related goals, to increase inclusivity and audience reach (e.g. targeting more women or youth), or to make workouts more engaging and challenging.
        </div>
      </li>

      <!-- Athletes -->
      <li class="flex items-start gap-4">
        <span class="flex-none mt-2 flex items-center justify-center w-5 h-5 rounded-full bg-vs-blue-900 shadow-2xs" aria-hidden="true">
          <span class="w-2 h-2 rounded-full bg-vs-yellow-400"></span>
        </span>
        <div class="text-base md:text-lg">
          <strong class="font-bold text-vs-blue-900 block sm:inline sm:mr-1">Athletes:</strong>
          Virtual sports provide new avenues for athletes to become professional by specialising in a new (virtual) discipline, as has for instance been the case <a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class="hover:bg-vs-blue-100/30" href="https://escapecollective.com/online-bike-racers-are-earning-more-than-you-might-think/">within virtual cycling</a>.
          Recreational and recreational-competitive athletes also benefit from interactive feedback, controllable training conditions, guided and structured workouts, and access to virtual competitions within their athletic capabilities, without potential risks and costs such as accidents or travel.
        </div>
      </li>

      <!-- Technology Developers -->
      <li class="flex items-start gap-4">
        <span class="flex-none mt-2 flex items-center justify-center w-5 h-5 rounded-full bg-vs-blue-900 shadow-2xs" aria-hidden="true">
          <span class="w-2 h-2 rounded-full bg-vs-yellow-400"></span>
        </span>
        <div class="text-base md:text-lg">
          <strong class="font-bold text-vs-blue-900 block sm:inline sm:mr-1">Technology Developers:</strong>
          Developers of hardware and software are opened up to a new market holding great potential in the future. Virtual sports products and services must be developed that address both mass markets and high-end elite markets. Both of these emerging markets are uniquely attractive as they carry the legitimacy, image, and consuming audience (both participants and spectators) of the traditional sports upon which they innovate.
        </div>
      </li>

    </ul>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- Section 3: Future Directions -->
<section id="future-directions" class="mb-16 lg:mb-24 text-lg md:text-xl leading-relaxed">
  <div class="mb-8 lg:mb-10">
    <h2 class="text-3xl md:text-4xl font-bold {{ colors.headingsCustom or colors.headingsDefault }} tracking-tight">
      Future Directions
    </h2>
  </div>

  <div class="space-y-6 lg:text-pretty">
    <p>
      As explained within the concluding remarks of Fouquaert's (<a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class="hover:bg-vs-blue-100/30" href="https://biblio.ugent.be/publication/01KWESKD8T405VTFE3PADRE2JB">2026</a>, Section 19.3) doctoral dissertation, 
      virtual sports are maturing from ad-hoc, "sport light", and commercial practices into institutionalised ways of practicing sport. To support this future growth and professionalisation, the industry and governing bodies must both reactively resolve <a class="hover:bg-vs-blue-100/30" href="/tags/integrity-issues/">contemporary issues in virtual sports</a>, while equally engaging proactively with future developments in the following areas:
    </p>

    <!-- Future Directions List -->
    <ul class="space-y-4 lg:space-y-6 leading-relaxed" role="list">
      
      <!-- Establishing Governance Structures -->
      <li class="flex items-start gap-4">
        <span class="flex-none mt-2 flex items-center justify-center w-5 h-5 rounded-full bg-vs-blue-900 shadow-2xs" aria-hidden="true">
          <span class="w-2 h-2 rounded-full bg-vs-yellow-400"></span>
        </span>
        <div class="text-base md:text-lg">
          <strong class="font-bold text-vs-blue-900 block sm:inline sm:mr-1">Establishing Governance Structures:</strong>
          To properly regulate and govern virtual sports, traditional sport governing bodies need to cooperate closely with commercial platform and hardware developers that possess the games' intellectual property.
          Formal structures, such as joint governance frameworks, agreements, and sector-wide regulatory networks, should be developed to safeguard sporting integrity.
        </div>
      </li>

      <!-- Implementing Standards -->
      <li class="flex items-start gap-4">
        <span class="flex-none mt-2 flex items-center justify-center w-5 h-5 rounded-full bg-vs-blue-900 shadow-2xs" aria-hidden="true">
          <span class="w-2 h-2 rounded-full bg-vs-yellow-400"></span>
        </span>
        <div class="text-base md:text-lg">
          <strong class="font-bold text-vs-blue-900 block sm:inline sm:mr-1">Implementing Standards:</strong>
          Whether it is about how athletes' performances are verified or about the way algorithms impact their performances, standards need to be established. 
          The industry would benefit from rules and structures that enable standard procedures and standard technological requirements, for example against <a href="/tags/cheating/" class="hover:bg-vs-blue-100/30">cheating in virtual sports</a>. Standards should be independently audited and certified, and open to any commercial developer to implement.
        </div>
      </li>

      <!-- Establishing Support Structures -->
      <li class="flex items-start gap-4">
        <span class="flex-none mt-2 flex items-center justify-center w-5 h-5 rounded-full bg-vs-blue-900 shadow-2xs" aria-hidden="true">
          <span class="w-2 h-2 rounded-full bg-vs-yellow-400"></span>
        </span>
        <div class="text-base md:text-lg">
          <strong class="font-bold text-vs-blue-900 block sm:inline sm:mr-1">Establishing Support Structures:</strong>
          Federations will have to develop support structures for their athletes, clubs, and coaches engaging with virtual sports. These structures will need to span all types of sport participation and sport organisation, from elite athletes and professional clubs to leisure participants and voluntary grassroots clubs. They could, for instance, include educational programs, coaching, or access to financial resources and assets.
        </div>
      </li>

      <!-- Balancing Policy Development -->
      <li class="flex items-start gap-4">
        <span class="flex-none mt-2 flex items-center justify-center w-5 h-5 rounded-full bg-vs-blue-900 shadow-2xs" aria-hidden="true">
          <span class="w-2 h-2 rounded-full bg-vs-yellow-400"></span>
        </span>
        <div class="text-base md:text-lg">
          <strong class="font-bold text-vs-blue-900 block sm:inline sm:mr-1">Balancing Policy Development:</strong>
          Institutional sport organisations must proactively balance the commercial, elite-oriented, and grassroots-oriented implementation of virtual sports. 
          Because virtual sports have the potential to promote the <a href="/tags/sport-for-all/" class="hover:bg-vs-blue-100/30">Sport-for-All policy goals</a> in innovative ways, it is important that policies and actual implementation are developed in a balanced way that benefits all sport logics and stakeholders.
        </div>
      </li>

    </ul>
  </div>
</section>